findings.recommendation:
Make each subcase seed its own precondition and drop the shared `config`, removing the ordering constraint entirely:

```go
testcases := []struct {
	name     string
	prefill  string // pattern installed before the case runs, "" for none
	answer   string
	want     string
}{
	{name: "set", answer: "example", want: "Filters: ok"},
	{name: "clear-existing", prefill: "example", answer: "", want: "Filters: regular expression cleared"},
	{name: "clear-absent", answer: "\n", want: "Filters: no filter on this column"},
	{name: "invalid-regexp", answer: "[0-", want: "Filters: error parsing regexp: missing closing ]: `[0-`"},
}

for _, tc := range testcases {
	t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
		config := newConfig()
		config.view = config.views["activity"]
		config.view.OrderKey = 0
		if tc.prefill != "" {
			config.view.Filters[0] = regexp.MustCompile(tc.prefill)
		}
		assert.Equal(t, tc.want, setFilter(tc.answer, config.view))
	})
}
```
Then the header comment about load-bearing order can be deleted. Add a `assert.Empty(t, config.view.Filters)` to `clear-absent` to pin that nothing was deleted on the no-op path.
